  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1143 PM CDT THU JUL 23 2026\n\nThe stalled cold front is still anticipated to ebb and flow\nacross the region through the weekend, keeping rain chances in\nthe forecast through Sunday. Given plenty of Gulf moisture still\nworking into the region, heavy rainfall is likely over the next\n48-hours as storms slowly move along the stalled front. Given the\nincreased risk for Flash Flooding, the Flood Watch remains in\neffect until Saturday for a handful of our northeast counties,\nwhere the highest rainfall amounts are expected.\n\nA significant pattern shift is still expected to take place by\nthe start of next week, as a stout upper-level ridge over the\nPlains begins to shift southeast. Afternoon high temperatures\nwill quickly climb into the mid and upper-90s by Monday,\nlingering through the middle of next week. Some guidance even has\na large swath of triple-digit afternoon highs on Tuesday. Given\nrecent rainfall, triple-digit temperatures are likely a bit\noverdone, but confidence is already high that heat index values\nwill surpass 105 degrees. Because of this, heat headlines already\nappear likely come the start of the new workweek.\n\n/44/",
